        <body>
            <div xml:id="d224">

                <pb facs="d224-01" n="1" xml:id="d224-01"/>

                <head>224. Briefing Memorandum From the Assistant Secretary of State for African
                    Affairs (Easum) to the Under Secretary of State (Sisco)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d224-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d224-f2"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1974-07-01"
                        >July 1, 1974</date>
                </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#underline">U.S. Reaction to Sudanese Decision
                        re Eight Palestinian Terrorists</hi></p>
                <p>I am attaching a paper which discusses the options available to us in dealing
                    with President Nimeiri’s decision to release the Black September terrorists to
                    the PLO. The paper is intended to provide a basis for your meeting with
                    Ambassador Brewer, me and others at 3 P.M. this afternon. Following that
                    meeting, we will refine the paper further in light of your comments.</p>



                <xi:include href="d224-a1.xml" xpointer="d224-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d224-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d224-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, RG 59, Central Policy Files, 1974, P850149–0644.
                    Secret; Exdis. Drafted by Smith, cleared in L/AF, NEA/EGY, S/CCT, AF, and
                    AF/E.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d224-f2" n="2" xml:id="d224-f2-content">
                <p> Easum sent Sisco a background paper on the commutation of the sentences of the
                    BSO terrorists and four options for a U.S. reaction, ranging from a temporary
                    freeze to a complete break in diplomatic relations.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
